        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Siege of Jerusalem (1099)</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">7 June to 15 July 1099; a first assault failed on 13 June and the city was entered on 15 July</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, then under Fatimid rule, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> under leaders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, Robert of Normandy, Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred</a>, with 12,000 to 14,000 men reaching the city, of whom 1,200 to 1,300 were cavalry; the Fatimid garrison under the governor Iftikhar ad-Dawla, roughly a thousand Arab and Sudanese troops and some 400 Egyptian elite cavalry</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The crusaders entered the city on 15 July and thousands of Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were massacred. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Advocatus Sancti Sepulchri</a> a week later, and a Fatimid army was beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> in August.</span></div>
