    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1095</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> calls for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1098</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> falls to the crusaders, who then defeat a Seljuk relief army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerbogha">Kerbogha</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1099</span><span class="rf-text">The army reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 7 June and its first assault, on 13 June, fails</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1099</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> march barefoot to the Mount of Olives on 8 July after a vision reported by Peter Desiderius</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1099</span><span class="rf-text">The final assault begins on 14 July and crusaders enter the city on 15 July, followed by a massacre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1099</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> is made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Advocatus Sancti Sepulchri</a> on 22 July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1099</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey</a> defeats a Fatimid army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ascalon">Battle of Ascalon</a> on 12 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1100</span><span class="rf-text">Baldwin is crowned the first King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> day</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="siege-of-jerusalem-1099" data-v="7764022e5b" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The crusaders take up positions round the walls</b> The crusaders could not surround the whole city, so they placed themselves where the walls could be approached. Normandy, Flanders and Godfrey faced the north and north-west, Raymond lay to the south, and the east was left unguarded.</li><li><b>Raymond moves up to Mount Zion</b> The valley kept Raymond too far from the walls, so he moved his army up to Mount Zion after two or three days. The army then stood on the hill south-west of the wall.</li><li><b>The first assault fails</b> The crusaders attacked the walls with whatever materials they could find. The attack had some early success but failed, which showed that they needed siege engines.</li><li><b>The armies move into place for the final attack</b> After almost three weeks of building, the ram and the siege tower were ready. Godfrey moved east behind the ram, the tower came up from Mount Zion, and Iftikhar moved his troops to the north wall.</li><li><b>The ram and the tower go forward</b> To the sound of trumpets the crusaders attacked from north and south. Normandy and Flanders shot at the walls, the ram was brought up to the wall, and the tower advanced from Mount Zion while the defenders fired back.</li><li><b>The ram breaks through but sticks</b> The ram pierced the wall in the north, but it stuck and the crusaders could not use the breach. They abandoned it, and the general attack was stopped.</li><li><b>The tower is pushed up against the wall</b> The attack was resumed at midday on Friday 15 July. The southern army pushed the siege tower right up against the wall, after the ram had been abandoned the day before.</li><li><b>The crusaders enter the city</b> The crusaders were able to enter the city, with Godfrey and Tancred in the vanguard. Near Mount Zion the defenders still fought fiercely against Raymond's men.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Urban II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urbano_II_in_consacrazione_de_l%27altare_del_monastero_di_Cluny_(detail)_(crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1095 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> called for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a>. Besides aiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> threatened, its goal was to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> and liberate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The crusaders left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in April 1097 and reached northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the autumn. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Boulogne</a> marched east and in the spring of 1098 founded the County of Edessa, while the main army besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> from October 1097. After the city fell in June 1098 and a Seljuk relief army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerbogha">Kerbogha</a> was beaten, the advance came to a standstill.</p><p>Rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch">Bohemond of Taranto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond IV of Toulouse</a>, epidemics and shortages of supplies delayed it further. Raymond took command and resumed the march south in January 1099. Following the coast, the troops avoided long sieges of fortified cities and instead made agreements with local Muslim rulers or paid tribute. At the end of May the army left the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and reached the neighbourhood of Jerusalem on 7 June.</p><p>The city had been out of Christian control since the Muslim conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in 637. It had been held for a century first by the Seljuk Turks and later by the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, who had ruled it since the 10th century.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Siege of Jerusalem (1099)&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude, Situation within the city, Situation outside the city:</p><ul><li>Steven Runciman, The First Crusade (Cambridge University Press, 1957)</li><li>Conor Kostick, The Siege of Jerusalem (Bloomsbury, 2009)</li><li>Thomas S. Asbridge, The First Crusade A New History: The Roots of Conflict between Christianity and Islam (Oxford University Press, 2004)</li><li>David Nicolle, The First Crusade 1096-1099: Conquest of the Holy Land (Osprey Publishing, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The crusader army had been estimated at 20,000 to 75,000 troops assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>. Between 12,000 and 14,000 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 7 June, among them 1,200 to 1,300 cavalry. The garrison comprised roughly a thousand Arab and Sudanese troops and some 400 Egyptian elite cavalry under the governor, Iftikhar ad-Dawla. He had denied the crusaders water, food and wood near the city, expelled Syrian Christians who might sympathise with them and allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to stay. He had all available <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and rope used for more mangonels and ordered bags of straw to be lowered on ropes beside the walls to soften the impact of stones.</p><p>The walls were about four kilometres long, three metres thick and 15 metres high, with five major gates each guarded by a pair of towers. Steep valleys protected three sides, and only the north wall and the south-west over Mount Zion offered ground favourable to an attacker. A deep ditch dug by Seljuk governors ringed much of the city, and an outer wall just inside it, linked to the city at several points, was meant to block the low flight of stones thrown by mangonels.</p><p>With too few men to besiege the whole city, the crusaders concentrated on the approachable sectors. Robert of Normandy held the north wall opposite the Gate of Flowers, with Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> facing the Gate of the Column. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred</a> covered the north-west corner down to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> Gate, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> stood to the south, moving up to Mount Zion after two or three days. The eastern and south-eastern sectors were left unguarded.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond</a> of Aguilers, a prophecy of an old hermit led to the first attack: if the crusaders attacked on 13 June until the ninth hour, the city would fall to them. The army improvised equipment overnight and, despite early success, the attack failed. Two days later the leaders met and concluded that the situation favoured the defenders. Thirst and ambushes while seeking water were taking lives, and rumours said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal-shahanshah">al-Afdal</a> was gathering a relief army, which left about a fortnight to take the city.</p><p>On 17 June the crusaders learned that English and Genoese ships had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, bringing materials for siege equipment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timber</a> was found over 50 kilometres away near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-embriaco">Guglielmo Embriaco</a> and Gaston of Béarn they built two wheel-mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a>, an iron-headed ram, scaling ladders and wattle screens in almost three weeks. On 8 July the whole army walked barefoot to the Mount of Olives, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and priests, after Peter Desiderius reported a vision of the legate Adhémar calling for a fast and a procession.</p><p>The assault began in the night of 13 to 14 July, with the main attack from Mount Zion and on the eastern part of the north wall and a feint at the north-west angle. By evening the ram had pierced the north wall, but it stuck, and the general attack was stopped. At midday on Friday 15 July the attack resumed; the tower was pushed against the wall and the crusaders entered the city. Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> says a golden cross was placed on top of the tower.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Siege of Jerusalem (1099)&#8221; cites where it covers Siege, Construction of siege engines, Penitential procession, Final attack:</p><ul><li>Conor Kostick, The Siege of Jerusalem (Bloomsbury, 2009)</li><li>Steven Runciman, The First Crusade (Cambridge University Press, 1957)</li><li>Adrian Boas, Jerusalem In The Time Of The Crusades (Routledge, 2001)</li><li>Thomas S. Asbridge, The First Crusade A New History: The Roots of Conflict between Christianity and Islam (Oxford University Press, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock.jpg" alt="Dome of the Rock" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_DomeoftheRock_J65_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ludvig14, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The crusaders' losses in the final attack were heavy, probably a fifth or a quarter of the Western armies. After the city fell, thousands of Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were massacred. Accounts by Christians, Muslims and Jews all describe women and children not being spared, and eyewitnesses say the blood on the Temple Mount reached to the ankles or the reins. Jews who had defended the city alongside the Muslims were burned alive in their synagogue. Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> mentions a second mass execution days later of those first spared for ransom.</p><p>Some Muslims withdrew to the Tower of David, and after negotiations Iftikhar and others left safely for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>. Jews were also spared if they left the city voluntarily. Muslim holy sites on the Temple Mount were converted into Christian shrines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond</a> of Aguilers writes of heads, hands and feet lying in the houses and streets, and of men and knights running to and fro over the corpses.</p><p>There are no reliable figures for the dead. Contemporary Arab sources give 70,000 or even 100,000, which is unrealistic against a population of about 30,000, and modern estimates range from 3,000 to 10,000. On 17 July a council met on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> about who should be king. On 22 July <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Advocatus Sancti Sepulchri</a> and declined the title of king, refusing to wear a crown of gold where Christ wore one of thorns. He defeated a Fatimid army at Ascalon on 12 August.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Siege of Jerusalem (1099)&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Founding of the Latin Kingdom:</p><ul><li>Christopher Tyerman, God's War: A New History of the Crusades (Harvard University Press, 2006)</li><li>Conor Kostick, The Siege of Jerusalem (Bloomsbury, 2009)</li><li>Adrian Boas, Jerusalem In The Time Of The Crusades (Routledge, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The siege is known chiefly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond</a> of Aguilers, the anonymous authors known as the Anonymi and Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> are called eyewitnesses; Ralph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> also reports events of the siege. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> wrote about 90 years after the massacre. Christian, Muslim and Jewish accounts all describe the bloodshed, though the Christian eyewitnesses regarded it as a triumph of God and express no regret, which later accounts such as William's do.</p><p>Much is disputed or told by one chronicler only. Raymond of Aguilers is the source for the hermit's prophecy, and Albert of Aachen for the golden cross and for a local Christian who showed the crusaders where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> lay. Ralph of Caen has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred</a> finding a cave with about 400 beams, left perhaps by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. Visions such as that of Peter Desiderius are reported as what he claimed. The death toll is the largest uncertainty. Arab figures of 70,000 or 100,000 are not credible against a population of about 30,000, and modern estimates run from 3,000 to 10,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-crusade.jpg" alt="First Crusade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_the_Hermit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> marked the successful end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> and led soon afterwards to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a>, who had played the most fundamental role in the conquest, took the title of advocate of the Holy Sepulchre and held it until his death on 18 July 1100. His brother Baldwin was asked to come to Jerusalem to take over his inheritance, in order to prevent Daibert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> from taking the city. Baldwin arrived in November 1100 and was crowned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> day in the Church of the Nativity as the first King of Jerusalem.</p><p>The fall of the city also gave Christian rulers control of Jerusalem for the first time since 637. The Temple Mount's Muslim holy sites became Christian shrines.</p><p>The accounts agree on the bloodshed. Atrocities against the inhabitants of cities taken by storm were normal in ancient and medieval warfare, by Christians and Muslims alike. The crusaders had already done so at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> had done so at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rometta">Rometta</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. The eyewitnesses saw the bloodshed as a triumph of God, not the killing of innocents.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>From 7 June to 15 July 1099 the crusaders besieged Fatimid-held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, took it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> after a failed first assault, and massacred thousands of its Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Modern estimates of the dead run from 3,000 to 10,000. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> followed.</em></p>

